{{smaller block|<poem>Yf thai nyl bere, a ''wegge'' oute of a bronde
Ywrought dryve in the roote, or sumdel froo
Let diche and fild with asshen let it stonde.</poem>
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 53.}}}}
{{smaller block|Thorw wich pyn ther goth a litel ''wegge'' which that is cleped the hors. {{right|''Chaucer'', Astrolabe.}}}}
{{smaller block|For ’tis with Pleasure as it is with ''Wedges;'' one drives out another.
{{right|''N. Bailey'', tr. of Colloquies of Erasmus, I. 157.}}}}
'''2.''' A mass resembling a wedge in form; anything in the form of a wedge.
{{smaller block|They gather it [gold] with great laboure and melte it and caste it, fyrste into masses or ''wedges'', and afterwarde into brode plates.
''R. Eden'', tr. of Sebastian Munster (First Books on Amer-
{{right|[ica, ed. Arber, p. 29).}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Open the mails, yet guard the treasure sure;
Lay out our golden ''wedges'' to the view.</poem>
{{right|''Marlowe'', Tamburlaine, I., i. 12.}}}}
{{smaller block|A ''wedge'' of gold of fifty shekels weight. {{right|Josh. vii. 21.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>See how in warlike muster they appear,
In rhombs, and ''wedges'', and half-moons, and wings.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. R., iii. 309.}}}}
'''3.''' In ''her.'', a bearing representing a triangle with one very acute angle—that is, like a pile, but free in the escutcheon instead of being attached to one of its edges.—'''4.''' In Cambridge University, the name given to the man whose name stands lowest on the list of the classical tripos: said to be a designation suggested by the name (Wedgewood) of the man who occu-

