pied this place on the first list (1824). Compare ''wooden spoon'', under ''spoon''<sup>1</sup>.
{{smaller block|Five were Wranglers, four of these Double men, and the fifth a favorite for the ''Wedge''. The last man is called the ''Wedge'', corresponding to the Spoon in Mathematics.
{{right|''C. A. Bristed'', English University, p. 312.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Foxtail wedge.''' Same as ''fox-wedge''.—'''The thin''' or '''small end of the wedge,''' figuratively, an initiatory move of small apparent importance, but calculated to produce or lead to an ultimate important effect.—'''Wedge of least resistance,''' the form in which loose earth and other substances yield to pressure.—'''Wooden wedge.''' Same as ''wedge''<sup>1</sup>, 4.}}

¶
'''wedge'''<sup>1</sup> (wej), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''wedged'', ppr. ''wedging''. [< late ME. ''wedgen''; from the noun.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To cleave with a wedge or with wedges; rive.
{{smaller block|<poem>My heart,
As ''wedged'' with a sigh, would rive in twain.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. and C., i. 1. 35.}}}}
'''2.''' To drive as a wedge is driven; crowd or compress closely; jam.
{{smaller block|<poem>Among the crowd i’ the Abbey; where a finger
Could not be ''wedged'' in more.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. VIII., iv. 1. 58.}}
<poem>''Wedged'' in the rocky shoals, and sticking fast.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Æneid, v. 285.}}
The age had not so much refinement that any sense of impropriety restrained the wearers of petticoat and farthingale from stepping forth into the public ways, and ''wedging'' their not unsubstantial persons . . . into the throng nearest to the scaffold at an execution.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Scarlet Letter, ii.}}}}

