'''Wednesday''' (wenz′dā), ''n.'' [< ME. ''Wednesday'', ''Wodnesdei'', ''Wednesdai'', < AS. ''Wōdnes dæg'' = D. ''Woensdag'' = Icel. ''Othinsdagr'' = Sw. Dan. ''Onsdag'' (for *''Odensdag''); lit. ‘Woden’s day’: AS. ''Wōdnes'', gen. of ''Wōden'' = OS. ''Wōdan'', ''Wōden'' = OHG. ''Wuotan'', ''Wōtan'' = Icel. ''Ōthinn'' (> E. ''Odin''), Woden; prob. lit. ‘the furious,’ i. e., the mighty warrior, < AS. ''wod'', etc., furious, raging, mad: see ''wood''<sup>2</sup>.] The fourth day of the week; the day next after Tuesday. Abbreviated ''W.'', ''Wed.'' See ''week''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Pulver Wednesday†.''' {{smaller|Same as ''Ash Wednesday''.}}

'''wedset†''', ''v. t.'' [ME. ''wedsetten''; < ''wed'' + ''set''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''wadset''.] To pledge: same as ''wadset''.

'''wee'''<sup>1</sup> (wē), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [< ME. ''we'', in the phrase ''a little we'', a little bit, a short way or space, appar. for ''a little way'', the form ''we'' being appar. a Scand. form (Icel. ''vegr'', a way, = Sw. ''väg'' = Dan. ''vei'') of ''way'': see ''way''<sup>1</sup>. ''Little'' and ''wee'' were and are so constantly associated that they have become synonymous, and ''wee'' has changed to an adjective. Cf. ''way-bit'', equiv. to ''wee bit''. E. ''wee'' cannot be connected with OHG. ''wenac'', G. ''wenig'', little.] '''I.''' ''n.'' A bit. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') A short distance.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Behynd hir a litill ''we''
It [a stone] fell.</poem>
{{right|''Barbour'', Bruce (E. E. T. S.), xvii. 677.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') A short space of time.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>O hold your hand, you minister,
Hold it a little ''wee''.</poem>
{{right|''Sweet William'' (Child’s Ballads, IV. 263).}}}}
'''II.''' ''a.'' Small; little; tiny. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|He hath but a little ''wee'' face, with a little yellow beard.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., i. 4. 22.}}}}

