'''wee<sup>2</sup>†''', ''n.'' An obsolete form of ''woe.''

'''wee<sup>3</sup>†''', ''pron.'' An old spelling of ''we.''

'''weebit''' (wē′bit), ''n.'' Same as ''way-bit.''

'''weechelm†''', ''n.'' An obsolete form of ''witch-elm.''

'''weed<sup>1</sup>''' (wēd), ''n.'' [< ME. ''weed, wed, weod, wied,'' a weed, < AS. ''wéod, wiod'' = OS. MD. ''wiod,'' D. ''wiede,'' a weed, = LG. ''woden, woen,'' pl., the green stalks and leaves of turnips, etc.] '''1.''' Any one of those herbaceous plants which are useless and without special beauty, or especially which are positively troublesome. {{smaller|The application of this general term is somewhat relative. Handsome but pernicious plants, as the oxeye daisy, cone-flower, and the purple cow-wheat of Europe (''Melampyrum arvense''), are weeds to the agriculturist, flowers to the esthetic. So also plants that are cultivated for use or beauty, as grasses, hemp, carrot, parsnip, morning-glory, become weeds when they spring up where they are not wanted. The exotics of cool countries are sometimes weeds in the tropics.}}
{{smaller block|On fat londe and ful of donge foulest ''wedes'' groweth.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), xiii. 224.}}}}
{{smaller block|An ill ''weed'' grows apace. ''Beau. and Fl.'', Coxcomb, iv. 3.}}
'''2.''' A sorry, worthless animal unfit for the breeding of stock; especially, a leggy, loose-bodied horse; a race-horse having the appearance but wanting the other qualities of a thoroughbred. [Slang.]
{{smaller block|He bore the same relation to a man of fashion that a ''weed'' does to a “winner of the Derby.”
{{right|''Lever'', Davenport Dunn, ii.}}}}
'''3.''' A cigar; with the definite article, tobacco. [Colloq.]
